+4. Test the samples: run the program via bash on the sample inputs from the problem statement and check the outputs. For interactive problems, write a small local judge/simulator and run your solution against it.
+   Canonical answer analysis: even when the statement says "print any" or allows multiple answers, the grader may expect one specific canonical answer (e.g. the lexicographically smallest, the first found in a natural enumeration order, or a greedy choice). Compare the sample outputs against candidate canonical rules, pick the rule that reproduces ALL samples, and make the solution output exactly that canonical answer. Do not rely on the checker accepting any valid answer.
+5. Write scratch test files OUTSIDE the repository source (e.g. in a separate scratch directory):
+   a. brute.py: an obviously-correct brute-force reference (e.g. exhaustive search) that prints the canonical output in exactly the same format as the solution.
+   b. gen.py: a random input generator that accepts a seed argument and covers several size regimes: tiny inputs, medium inputs with many duplicates / repeated values / ties, and boundary values (min/max values, n=1, all-equal elements, extreme values).
+   c. Run a compare loop on EXACT output equality (not just validity) for at least 300 cases per regime, including medium sizes with many duplicates. The `stress_test` tool does this: pass solution_cmd, brute_cmd, gen_cmd, iterations (>=300) and exact_match=true. Run it once per regime (e.g. the generator takes a regime argument, or use separate gen scripts).
+   d. Investigate every mismatch. If the solution differs from brute.py, fix the solution (or the brute force if the brute force is wrong) and rerun all regimes.
+6. Only after all correctness tests pass, run timing tests at maximum constraints (largest n, largest values) and make sure the solution finishes well within the time limit. Optimize if it is too slow.
+7. Investigate any stderr output or lines starting with "Error:" from your runs instead of dismissing them. A traceback or error message usually points to a real bug (e.g. an edge case, wrong input parsing, or a recursion limit); find its cause and fix it.
+8. Finally verify with `git status` and `git diff` that the source file has actually changed. Fix any problems you find.
 
